			<div class=3D"content"><blockquote><div><cite>Ed Dyreen wrote:</cite>Whe=
re can I find some in depth information on the LF and EOL tricks.<br></div>=
</blockquote><br>Somewhere, sometime I have posted something about that, bu=
t I can't remember where  <img class=3D"smilies" src=3D"https://www.dostips=
.com/forum/images/smilies/icon_sad.gif" alt=3D":(" title=3D"Sad"> <br><br>B=
ut there are only a few simple rules.<br>A percent-expanded &lt;LF&gt; char=
acter in a normal line stops the parser.<br>A percent-expanded &lt;LF&gt; i=
n a bracket starts a new command line<br>A delayed or %%var expanded &lt;LF=
&gt; is handled as normal character<br>To escape a LF with a caret there is=
 the rule:<br>The first &lt;LF&gt; after a caret is ignored, the next chara=
cter after the &lt;LF&gt; is escaped even if this is also a &lt;LF&gt;<br>T=
herefore you need two empty lines to create a LF, the &lt;LF&gt; after the =
caret is removed, the next &lt;LF&gt; is escaped, so the second line doesn'=
t have an end, therefore you need the second empty line, else you append ru=
bbish.<br><br><div class=3D"codebox"><p>Code: <a href=3D"https://www.dostip=
s.com/forum/viewtopic.php?f=3D3&amp;t=3D1827#">Select all</a></p><pre><code=
>set LF=3D^<br><br><br>rem ** Two empty lines are neccessary<br>set recreat=
eLF=3D^%LF%%LF%<br>REM The contents of the variables recreateLF and LF are =
both a single linefeed<br>setlocal EnableDelayedExpansion<br>echo 1a: Line1=
!LF!1b: Line2<br>echo 2a: hallo%LF%2b: this is lost<br>(<br>echo 3a: hallo%=
lf%echo 3b: this is a legal command<br>)<br>set var1=3Dthis!LF!works<br>set=
 "var2=3Dthis!LF!works too"<br>set var3=3Dthis%LF%fails<br>set "var4=3Dthis=
%LF%fails too"<br>set var5=3Dthis^%LF%%LF%works<br>set "var6=3Dthis^%LF%%LF=
%fails"<br>set ^"var7=3Dthis^%LF%%LF%works again"<br>set var<br></code></pr=
e></div><br>Output<div class=3D"codebox"><p>Code: <a href=3D"https://www.do=
stips.com/forum/viewtopic.php?f=3D3&amp;t=3D1827#">Select all</a></p><pre><=
code>1a: Line1<br>1b: Line2<br>2a: hallo<br>3a: hallo<br>3b: this is a lega=
l command<br>var1=3Dthis<br>works<br>var2=3Dthis<br>works too<br>var3=3Dthi=
s<br>var4=3Dthis<br>var5=3Dthis<br>works<br>var6=3Dthis^<br>var7=3Dthis<br>=
works again</code></pre></div><br><br><blockquote><div><cite>Ed Dyreen wrot=
e:</cite>what does xlf do in set ^"xlf=3D^^^%lf%%lf%^%lf%%lf%" ?</div></blo=
ckquote><br>xlf is the variable name, the name isn't important.<br>The cont=
ent of XLF is "^&lt;LF&gt;&lt;LF&gt;" (3-characters) so you can use it with=
 percent-expansion.<br>echo Line1%XLF%Line2<br><br><blockquote><div><cite>E=
d Dyreen wrote:</cite>why is the first quote escaped, is there an inbalance=
 in quotes, I don't understand <img class=3D"smilies" src=3D"https://www.do=
stips.com/forum/images/smilies/icon_eek.gif" alt=3D":shock:" title=3D"Shock=
ed"></div></blockquote><br>No it isn't unbalanced, I could also write <span=
 style=3D"color: #4000FF">set xlf=3D^^^%lf%%lf%^%lf%%lf%</span> but I prefe=
r quotes to ensure a proper end without "hidden" spaces.<br>But here a norm=
al quote would fail, as it quotes the carets and then the &lt;LF&gt;s aren'=
t escaped anymore, therefore I escape the first quote.<br><br>jeb</div>

		=09
